window.BPHO_QUESTIONS = (window.BPHO_QUESTIONS || []).concat([

{
  id: "S06-01", module: "H", topic: "A voltmeter of finite resistance loads the potential divider it is measuring, so the reading is below the unloaded value", diff: 3, rel: [["H", "Resistors in parallel combine as the reciprocal of the sum of reciprocals, so a pair is always smaller than either"], ["H", "The potential divider rule divides the supply in the ratio of the resistances in series"], ["H", "Ohm's law applied to the loaded circuit as a whole, and then to the pair"], ["A", "A measurement can disturb the thing measured, and the size of the disturbance depends on the instrument"]], key: ["voltmeter", "loading", "divider", "parallel"], ans: 0, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["H", "Resistors in parallel combine as the reciprocal of the sum of reciprocals, so a pair is always smaller than either"], ["H", "The potential divider rule divides the supply in the ratio of the resistances in series"], ["H", "Ohm's law applied to the loaded circuit as a whole, and then to the pair"], ["A", "A measurement can disturb the thing measured, and the size of the disturbance depends on the instrument"]],
  q: `<p>The cell has negligible internal resistance. The voltmeter has a resistance of 24 &#937; and is connected across the 8.0 &#937; resistor, as the diagram shows.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 290" role="img" aria-label="A 12 volt cell in series with a 4.0 ohm and an 8.0 ohm resistor, with a voltmeter connected in parallel across the 8.0 ohm resistor">
<line x1="90" y1="70" x2="500" y2="70" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="90" y1="240" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="500" y1="70" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="90" y1="70" x2="90" y2="146" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="90" y1="160" x2="90" y2="240" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="76" y1="146" x2="104" y2="146" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="84" y1="160" x2="96" y2="160" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="4.5"/><text x="112" y="158" font-size="11.5" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">12 V</text><rect x="170" y="58" width="70" height="24" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="205" y="50" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">4.0 &#937;</text><rect x="340" y="58" width="70" height="24" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="375" y="50" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">8.0 &#937;</text><line x1="310" y1="70" x2="310" y2="150" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="310" y1="150" x2="358" y2="150" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="402" y1="150" x2="440" y2="150" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="440" y1="150" x2="440" y2="70" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="380" cy="150" r="22" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="380" y="156" font-size="12.5" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">V</text><circle cx="310" cy="70" r="3.6" fill="#14181f" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="0"/><circle cx="440" cy="70" r="3.6" fill="#14181f" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="0"/>
</svg>
</figure><p>What does the voltmeter read?</p>`,
  opts: ["7.2 V", "8.0 V", "6.0 V", "4.8 V", "2.4 V"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you treat the voltmeter as part of the circuit. A voltmeter of finite resistance takes current, and that current has to come through the 4.0 &#937; resistor, which means less voltage is left for the branch being measured.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the meter changes the branch it is across.</b> Before the meter is connected, the branch is 8.0 &#937;. With the meter across it, the branch is the 8.0 &#937; and the 24 &#937; in parallel. That combination is smaller than either, which is the whole point.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — combine the pair.</b></p>
<div class="formula">R_pair = 8.0 &#215; 24 / (8.0 + 24) = 192/32 = 6.0 &#937;</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the circuit resistance.</b> The pair is in series with the 4.0 &#937;:</p>
<div class="formula">R_total = 4.0 + 6.0 = 10 &#937;</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the supply current.</b></p>
<div class="formula">I = 12/10 = 1.2 A</div>
<p>Compare that with the unloaded current, 12/12 = 1.0 A. The meter has made the circuit draw <i>more</i> current, not less: it added a path, so the total resistance fell from 12 &#937; to 10 &#937;.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — the reading.</b> The meter reads the p.d. across the pair it is connected to:</p>
<div class="formula">V = I &#215; R_pair = 1.2 &#215; 6.0 = 7.2 V</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The reading is 7.2 V, which is below the unloaded 8.0 V. Loading must always pull the reading towards zero, never above it, so the direction of the change is right. A useful cross-check: the current through the 8.0 &#937; is 7.2/8.0 = 0.90 A and the current through the meter is 7.2/24 = 0.30 A, and 0.90 + 0.30 = 1.2 A, which is the supply current from Step 4.</p>
<p>The other four options are the readings four different mistakes give. Reporting 8.0 V means never connecting the meter at all. Reporting 6.0 V means reading the pair&#8217;s resistance in ohms as if it were a voltage. Reporting 4.8 V means reading across the wrong resistor. Reporting 2.4 V means multiplying the meter&#8217;s current by the 8.0 &#937; instead of by the meter&#8217;s own resistance.</p>
<p><b>Answer: A</b></p>`,
  trap: `The classic error is to answer with the unloaded divider value, 8.0 V, and never notice that the meter is a resistor. The second trap is subtler: the supply current RISES when the meter is connected (1.0 A becomes 1.2 A), so a candidate who assumes the meter can only reduce the current reaches a wrong number by a different route.`
},

{
  id: "S06-02", module: "A", topic: "Propagating a small fractional change through a square root, using the first-order expansion", diff: 2, rel: [["A", "The period of a simple pendulum depends on the square root of its length"], ["A", "For a small fractional change, the power law passes the fractional change through multiplied by the exponent"], ["A", "The first-order expansion of the square root of one plus a small quantity"], ["A", "Working in fractions rather than in absolute values, because only the ratio matters"]], key: ["smallchange", "powerlaw", "squareroot", "firstorder"], ans: 2, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["A", "The period of a simple pendulum depends on the square root of its length"], ["A", "For a small fractional change, the power law passes the fractional change through multiplied by the exponent"], ["A", "The first-order expansion of the square root of one plus a small quantity"], ["A", "Working in fractions rather than in absolute values, because only the ratio matters"]],
  q: `<p>A pendulum is timed and its period is T. Its length is then increased by 4.0 %.</p><p>By approximately what percentage does its period change?</p>`,
  opts: ["8.0 %", "4.0 %", "2.0 %", "1.0 %", "0.50 %"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you propagate a small change through a power law, rather than substituting numbers and doing the arithmetic. The 4.0 % is a hint: it is small, so the first-order expansion is the intended tool.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — write the dependence.</b> The period of a simple pendulum is</p>
<div class="formula">T = 2&#960; &#8730;(&#8467;/g)</div>
<p>Everything in front of the square root is constant, so the question is how the square root of &#8467; responds to a 4.0 % change in &#8467;.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — write the change as a factor.</b> If the length becomes &#8467;(1 + x) with x = 0.040, the new period is</p>
<div class="formula">T_new = T &#215; (1 + x) to the power one half</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — expand to first order.</b> For a small x,</p>
<div class="formula">(1 + x) to the power one half &#8776; 1 + x/2</div>
<p>This is the small-parameter approximation the BPhO constant sheet supplies, and the reason it supplies it is that questions like this are set. It is valid here because 0.040 is far smaller than 1.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — read off the change.</b> The new period is T(1 + 0.020), so the fractional change is</p>
<div class="formula">&#916;T/T &#8776; x/2 = 0.040/2 = 0.020</div>
<p>which is 2.0 %.</p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> A longer pendulum has a longer period, so the change must be an increase, and it is. And the change must be smaller than the 4.0 % that caused it, because a square root always shrinks a small fractional change; a quarter power would shrink it further, and a square would enlarge it.</p>
<p>Notice that the answer does not depend on g, on the original length, or on the original period. That is a good sign: a question that asks only for a percentage change should not need the absolute values, and if your method needs them you have made it harder than it is.</p>
<p><b>Answer: C</b></p>`,
  trap: `Two traps. The first is treating the period as proportional to the length, which gives 4.0 % and is the answer for a stretched spring rather than a pendulum. The second is remembering that a square root halves the change but applying the half in the wrong direction, which gives 8.0 %.`
},

{
  id: "S06-03", module: "C", topic: "A block pulled up a rough inclined plane at constant speed: resolving along the plane and getting the direction of friction right", diff: 3, rel: [["C", "On an inclined plane the weight resolves into a component along the plane and one perpendicular to it"], ["C", "Friction acts to oppose relative motion, so a block moving up the slope feels friction down the slope"], ["C", "The normal reaction is not the weight: it is the component of the weight perpendicular to the plane"], ["C", "At constant speed the forces along the plane balance, so the applied force equals the sum of the opposing ones"]], key: ["incline", "friction", "resolution", "equilibrium"], ans: 1, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["C", "On an inclined plane the weight resolves into a component along the plane and one perpendicular to it"], ["C", "Friction acts to oppose relative motion, so a block moving up the slope feels friction down the slope"], ["C", "The normal reaction is not the weight: it is the component of the weight perpendicular to the plane"], ["C", "At constant speed the forces along the plane balance, so the applied force equals the sum of the opposing ones"]],
  q: `<p>The block has a mass of 2.0 kg and is pulled up the slope at a constant speed by the force F shown. The coefficient of friction between the block and the slope is 0.25, and g is 10 N kg<sup>&#8722;1</sup>.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 290" role="img" aria-label="A block on a plane inclined at 30 degrees to the horizontal. The weight acts down, the normal reaction perpendicular to the plane, the friction down the slope, and the applied force F up the slope">
<defs><marker id="s0603rd" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#b3352f"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0603bl" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#2f5fd0"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0603gr" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#1f7a53"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0603am" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#a8641a"/></marker></defs><polygon points="110,250 420,71 420,250" fill="#eef2f8" stroke="none" stroke-width="0" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="70" y1="250" x2="500" y2="250" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="110" y1="250" x2="420" y2="71" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="420" y1="71" x2="420" y2="250" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="274.5,124.9 287.5,147.5 242.5,173.5 229.5,150.9" fill="#dfe8f6" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="258.5" y1="149.2" x2="258.5" y2="221" stroke="#b3352f" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0603rd)"/><line x1="258.5" y1="149.2" x2="223.5" y2="88.6" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0603bl)"/><line x1="258.5" y1="149.2" x2="206.5" y2="179.2" stroke="#1f7a53" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0603gr)"/><line x1="258.5" y1="149.2" x2="314.8" y2="116.7" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0603am)"/><polyline points="190,250 189.957,247.382 189.829,244.768 189.615,242.159 189.316,239.558 188.931,236.968 188.463,234.393 187.91,231.834 187.274,229.294 186.555,226.777 185.754,224.285 184.872,221.82 183.91,219.385 182.869,216.983 181.75,214.617 180.554,212.288 179.282,210" fill="none" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/><text x="168" y="242" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">30&#176;</text><text x="266" y="195" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#b3352f">W</text><text x="215" y="84" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#2f5fd0">N</text><text x="196" y="168" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#1f7a53">f</text><text x="296" y="100" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#a8641a">F</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>What is the magnitude of F?</p>`,
  opts: ["5.7 N", "14 N", "15 N", "24 N", "4.3 N"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Two things at once: whether you resolve the weight correctly, and whether you get the direction of friction right. The figure settles the second, so a wrong answer here is a wrong reading of the figure.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — resolve the weight.</b> The weight is</p>
<div class="formula">W = mg = 2.0 &#215; 10 = 20 N</div>
<p>On a plane inclined at 30&#176;, the component down the slope is W sin 30 and the component perpendicular to the slope is W cos 30. This is the step most often reversed: it is the <i>along</i> component that carries the sine, because at zero inclination the along component must vanish.</p>
<div class="formula">W_along = 20 sin 30 = 10 N      W_perp = 20 cos 30 = 17.3 N</div>
<p>The perpendicular component needs cos 30, and cos 30 = &#8730;3/2. With &#8730;3 &#8776; 1.73 that is 0.87, so 20 cos 30 &#8776; 17.3 N. &#8730;3 to two decimal places is one of the few numerical facts a non-calculator paper expects you to carry.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the normal reaction.</b> There is no acceleration perpendicular to the plane, so the normal reaction balances the perpendicular component. It is <i>not</i> the whole weight:</p>
<div class="formula">N = 20 cos 30 = 17.3 N</div>
<p>This is the step that separates 14 N from 15 N. Using N = W = 20 N would give a friction of 5.0 N.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the friction.</b></p>
<div class="formula">f = &#956;N = 0.25 &#215; 17.3 = 4.3 N</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — which way does the friction point?</b> The block is moving <i>up</i> the slope, so friction acts <i>down</i> it. That is what the green arrow in the figure shows. So along the plane there are two forces opposing the motion and one driving it.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — balance along the plane.</b> The speed is constant, so there is no acceleration and the forces along the plane must balance:</p>
<div class="formula">F = W_along + f = 10 + 4.3 = 14.3 N</div>
<p>To two significant figures, F is 14 N.</p>
<p><b>A sanity check on the alternatives.</b> If the friction were drawn up the slope &#8212; the error you make by thinking of the block as sliding down &#8212; you would subtract and get 10 &#8722; 4.3 = 5.7 N, which is less than the weight component alone and therefore cannot move the block up the slope at all. That is a physical argument that rules the option out without doing any arithmetic, and it is worth noticing that the paper rewards noticing it.</p>
<p><b>Answer: B</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the direction of friction. A block sliding DOWN a slope feels friction UP it, and candidates carry that picture over. Here the block is pulled up, so friction points down and the two forces add. A second trap is using the whole weight as the normal reaction, which gives 15 N.`
},

{
  id: "S06-04", module: "H", topic: "A circuit with a nested parallel pair and internal resistance: working from the supply down to the current in one innermost resistor", diff: 3, rel: [["H", "Two resistors in parallel combine to less than either, by the reciprocal rule"], ["H", "A parallel combination in series with a resistor adds to it"], ["H", "The internal resistance of a cell is in series with the external circuit and carries the whole supply current"], ["H", "The p.d. across a branch is the current in that branch times the resistance of that branch, applied at each stage of the reduction"], ["C", "Power dissipated in a resistor is the square of the current times the resistance"]], key: ["nestedparallel", "internalresistance", "stagedreduction", "power"], ans: 3, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["H", "Two resistors in parallel combine to less than either, by the reciprocal rule"], ["H", "A parallel combination in series with a resistor adds to it"], ["H", "The internal resistance of a cell is in series with the external circuit and carries the whole supply current"], ["H", "The p.d. across a branch is the current in that branch times the resistance of that branch, applied at each stage of the reduction"], ["C", "Power dissipated in a resistor is the square of the current times the resistance"]],
  q: `<p>The cell has an e.m.f. of 12 V and an internal resistance of 2.0 &#937;. The connecting wires have negligible resistance.</p><p>The external circuit is a 5.0 &#937; resistor in series with two parallel branches. One branch is a single 6.0 &#937; resistor. The other branch is a 4.0 &#937; resistor in series with a parallel pair made of 6.0 &#937; and 3.0 &#937;.</p><p>What is the power dissipated in the 3.0 &#937; resistor?</p>`,
  opts: ["0.24 W", "1.4 W", "0.080 W", "0.48 W", "0.16 W"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> A reduction long enough that it cannot be done in one step, and a question that asks for something deep inside it. The numbers are chosen so that every stage is exact, which is what makes it a non-calculator question.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the innermost pair.</b> The 6.0 &#937; and the 3.0 &#937; are in parallel:</p>
<div class="formula">6.0 &#215; 3.0/(6.0 + 3.0) = 18/9 = 2.0 &#937;</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the second branch.</b> That pair is in series with the 4.0 &#937;:</p>
<div class="formula">4.0 + 2.0 = 6.0 &#937;</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the parallel section.</b> The second branch is 6.0 &#937; and the first branch is also 6.0 &#937;, and they are in parallel:</p>
<div class="formula">6.0 &#215; 6.0/(6.0 + 6.0) = 3.0 &#937;</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the circuit resistance.</b> The parallel section is in series with the 5.0 &#937; and with the cell&#8217;s own 2.0 &#937;:</p>
<div class="formula">R = 2.0 + 5.0 + 3.0 = 10 &#937;</div>
<p>The internal resistance must be included. It carries the whole supply current, so leaving it out would overstate every current in the circuit by twenty per cent.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — the supply current.</b></p>
<div class="formula">I = &#949;/R = 12/10 = 1.2 A</div>
<p><b>Step 6 — the p.d. across the parallel section.</b></p>
<div class="formula">V_section = 1.2 &#215; 3.0 = 3.6 V</div>
<p><b>Step 7 — the current in the second branch.</b> Both branches sit across 3.6 V, and the second branch is 6.0 &#937;:</p>
<div class="formula">I_branch = 3.6/6.0 = 0.60 A</div>
<p>As a check, the first branch is 3.6/6.0 = 0.60 A as well, and 0.60 + 0.60 = 1.2 A, which is the supply current. The two branches are equal, so the supply current splits equally, and that is a good sign the reduction was right.</p>
<p><b>Step 8 — the p.d. across the innermost pair.</b> Within the second branch, the 4.0 &#937; takes 0.60 &#215; 4.0 = 2.4 V, so the pair takes the rest:</p>
<div class="formula">V_pair = 0.60 &#215; 2.0 = 1.2 V</div>
<p><b>Step 9 — the current in the 3.0 &#937; resistor.</b> The pair sits across 1.2 V, and the 3.0 &#937; is one arm of it:</p>
<div class="formula">I = 1.2/3.0 = 0.40 A</div>
<p>The other arm carries 1.2/6.0 = 0.20 A, and 0.40 + 0.20 = 0.60 A, which is the branch current. Every junction adds up, which is the check that the whole chain of p.d.s was walked correctly.</p>
<p><b>Step 10 — the power.</b></p>
<div class="formula">P = I&#178;R = 0.40 &#215; 0.40 &#215; 3.0 = 0.48 W</div>
<p>Notice how easy it is to answer for the wrong resistor: the 6.0 &#937; in the pair dissipates 0.20&#178; &#215; 6.0 = 0.24 W, and the branch current 0.60 A applied to the 4.0 &#937; gives 1.4 W. The question names the 3.0 &#937;, and the only way to be sure you have it is to walk the chain as above.</p>
<p><b>Answer: D</b></p>`,
  trap: `Two traps. Leaving out the internal resistance gives 12/(5 + 3) = 1.5 A instead of 1.2 A, and every later number is then wrong. And answering for the neighbouring resistor is easy: the 6.0 &#937; in the same pair gives 0.24 W, which is one of the options. The question asks for the 3.0 &#937;, so the last division matters.`
},

{
  id: "S06-05", module: "A", topic: "An order-of-magnitude estimate assembled from a rate, a unit conversion and a lifetime", diff: 3, rel: [["A", "An estimate is built by multiplying rates and durations, each taken to the nearest convenient round number"], ["A", "Units must be made consistent before multiplying, and a rate per minute becomes a rate per year through two conversions"], ["A", "Rounding to the nearest power of ten is a decision made at the end, from the logarithm of the answer"], ["K", "Biological rates are quantities like any other, and a lifetime is a duration in the same sense as a half-life"]], key: ["estimation", "rate", "conversion", "poweroften"], ans: 4, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["A", "An estimate is built by multiplying rates and durations, each taken to the nearest convenient round number"], ["A", "Units must be made consistent before multiplying, and a rate per minute becomes a rate per year through two conversions"], ["A", "Rounding to the nearest power of ten is a decision made at the end, from the logarithm of the answer"], ["K", "Biological rates are quantities like any other, and a lifetime is a duration in the same sense as a half-life"]],
  q: `<p>A healthy adult heart beats about 70 times a minute.</p><p>Estimate, to the nearest power of ten, how many times a heart beats in a lifetime.</p>`,
  opts: ["10<sup>11</sup>", "10<sup>7</sup>", "10<sup>10</sup>", "10<sup>8</sup>", "10<sup>9</sup>"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can assemble an estimate from two quantities you have to supply yourself, and whether you know that "to the nearest power of ten" is a rounding decision rather than a guess.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what is the answer the product of?</b> Beats in a lifetime is a rate multiplied by a duration. Nothing else enters. Writing that down first is what stops the estimate drifting.</p>
<div class="formula">beats = rate &#215; lifetime</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the rate, in beats per year.</b> The rate is given per minute, so the conversion needs the number of minutes in a year:</p>
<div class="formula">60 &#215; 24 &#215; 365 &#8776; 60 &#215; 24 &#215; 400 = 5.8 &#215; 10 to the 5 minutes a year</div>
<p>Rounding the year to 400 days is a five per cent error, which is far below the precision the answer needs. Then</p>
<div class="formula">70 &#215; 5.8 &#215; 10 to the 5 &#8776; 4 &#215; 10 to the 7 beats a year</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the lifetime.</b> Eighty years is the usual round figure, and the exact value does not matter: fifty years and a hundred years differ by a factor of two, which is nowhere near a factor of ten.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — multiply.</b></p>
<div class="formula">4 &#215; 10 to the 7 &#215; 80 = 3.2 &#215; 10 to the 9</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — round to the nearest power of ten.</b> The boundary between two neighbouring powers of ten is not halfway in the ordinary sense. They are separated by a factor of ten, so the dividing line sits at &#8730;10 &#215; 10 to the 9. Since &#8730;10 &#8776; 3.16, the line is at 3.16 &#215; 10 to the 9, and the question is whether the estimate falls above it or below. Carrying the arithmetic exactly:</p>
<div class="formula">70 &#215; 60 &#215; 24 &#215; 365 &#215; 80 = 2.9 &#215; 10 to the 9</div>
<p>2.9 is below 3.16, so the answer is 10 to the 9. It is worth seeing how close to the line this sits: the rounded route above gave 3.2 &#215; 10 to the 9, which is on the far side of 3.16 and so tips over to 10 to the 10. That is the honest lesson of the question. An estimate of this kind is good to within a factor of a few, and near a boundary the factor of a few decides the answer.</p>
<p><b>Answer: E</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the rounding rule. 3 &#215; 10 to the 9 looks closer to 10 to the 10 than to 10 to the 9 if you compare the leading digit, but 3 is closer to 1 than to 10 in the multiplicative sense that a power-of-ten scale uses. The logarithm decides, not the digit.`
},

{
  id: "S06-06", module: "B", topic: "Average velocity from a velocity-time graph: the area under the graph divided by the whole time, expressed as a fraction of the peak speed", diff: 3, rel: [["B", "The area under a velocity-time graph is the displacement"], ["B", "Average velocity is the total displacement divided by the total time, not the mean of the speeds"], ["B", "A triangular region of a velocity-time graph contributes half the rectangle that encloses it"], ["A", "Expressing the answer as a fraction of a named quantity avoids committing to a value for it"]], key: ["grapharea", "averagevelocity", "trapezium", "fraction"], ans: 1, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["B", "The area under a velocity-time graph is the displacement"], ["B", "Average velocity is the total displacement divided by the total time, not the mean of the speeds"], ["B", "A triangular region of a velocity-time graph contributes half the rectangle that encloses it"], ["A", "Expressing the answer as a fraction of a named quantity avoids committing to a value for it"]],
  q: `<p>The graph shows the velocity of a cyclist over a 12 s interval. The speed rises uniformly from rest to 8.0 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup> in the first 4.0 s, stays approximately constant at 8.0 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup> until 10 s, and then falls uniformly to rest at 12 s.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 300" role="img" aria-label="A velocity-time graph rising linearly from zero to 8.0 metres per second in 4.0 seconds, constant at 8.0 for the next 6.0 seconds, then falling linearly to zero over the last 2.0 seconds">
<defs><marker id="s0606ar" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#14181f"/></marker></defs><polygon points="80,250 213.3,70 213.3,250" fill="#e6eefc" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="1" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polygon points="213.3,70 413.3,70 413.3,250 213.3,250" fill="#fdf3e0" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="1" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polygon points="413.3,70 480,250 413.3,250" fill="#e6eefc" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="1" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="80" y1="250" x2="510" y2="250" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0606ar)"/><line x1="80" y1="250" x2="80" y2="40" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0606ar)"/><polyline points="80,250 213.3,70 413.3,70 480,250" fill="none" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/><text x="80" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">0</text><text x="213.3" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">4.0</text><text x="413.3" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">10</text><text x="480" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">12</text><text x="72" y="74" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#14181f">8.0</text><text x="72" y="254" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#14181f">0</text><text x="86" y="34" font-size="11" fill="#14181f">velocity / m s<tspan font-size="9" dy="-4">-1</tspan></text><text x="540" y="288" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#14181f">time / s</text><text x="313" y="238" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">area = displacement</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>Taking the constant speed as v, what is the cyclist&#8217;s average velocity over the 12 s?</p>`,
  opts: ["v/2", "3v/4", "2v/3", "5v/6", "v"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you use the area under the graph rather than the peak, and whether you can carry the peak through as a symbol so that the arithmetic stays clean.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the rule.</b> Average velocity is the total displacement over the total time. The displacement is the area under a velocity-time graph, and the total time is the whole 12 s, including the parts where the cyclist is slow.</p>
<div class="formula">v_avg = area / total time</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — split the area.</b> The shape is a trapezium with a flat top, which splits into two triangles and a rectangle. The cyclist&#8217;s speed is approximately constant between 4.0 s and 10 s, so the middle region is a rectangle rather than a curve.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — each area in terms of v.</b></p>
<div class="formula">triangle 1: 0.5 &#215; 4.0 &#215; v = 2v
rectangle:  6.0 &#215; v = 6v
triangle 2: 0.5 &#215; 2.0 &#215; v = v</div>
<p>Each triangle contributes half of the rectangle that would enclose it. That is the discriminator of the figure: a candidate who counts each ramp as a rectangle gets 4v + 6v + 2v = 12v, and 12v/12 s = v, which is the last option.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — add and divide.</b></p>
<div class="formula">total area = 2v + 6v + v = 9v
v_avg = 9v / 12 = 3v/4</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Three quarters of the peak is below the peak, which it must be, because the cyclist is below the peak for four of the twelve seconds. And it is well above a half, because the cyclist spends half the interval at the full peak.</p>
<p>With the numbers put in, the displacement is 9 &#215; 8.0 = 72 m and the average velocity is 72/12 = 6.0 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>. Notice that the question did not need the 8.0: the answer is a fraction of v, and the fraction is the physics.</p>
<p><b>Answer: B</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is treating the average as the peak, which happens whenever the two ramps are counted as rectangles. The second trap is dividing by the time spent at the peak rather than by the whole interval: 72 m over 10 s gives 7.2 m s&#8722;1, which is also an option in a variant of this question.`
},

{
  id: "S06-07", module: "C", topic: "An elastic collision between equal masses, one at rest, where the two outgoing directions are symmetric: finding the angle", diff: 3, rel: [["C", "Momentum is conserved as a vector, so it must be conserved along the original direction and perpendicular to it"], ["C", "Kinetic energy is conserved in an elastic collision, which fixes the outgoing speeds"], ["A", "A collision with a symmetry axis can be resolved along that axis, turning a vector problem into two scalar ones"], ["A", "Dividing one conservation equation by another eliminates the unknown speed and leaves the angle"]], key: ["elastic", "momentum", "symmetry", "angle"], ans: 0, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["C", "Momentum is conserved as a vector, so it must be conserved along the original direction and perpendicular to it"], ["C", "Kinetic energy is conserved in an elastic collision, which fixes the outgoing speeds"], ["A", "A collision with a symmetry axis can be resolved along that axis, turning a vector problem into two scalar ones"], ["A", "Dividing one conservation equation by another eliminates the unknown speed and leaves the angle"]],
  q: `<p>A particle of mass m travelling with speed u strikes an identical particle at rest. The collision is elastic, and the two particles emerge symmetrically, each at the same angle &#952; to the original direction and each with the same speed v.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 290" role="img" aria-label="A particle of mass m moving with speed u towards an identical particle at rest. Two dashed outgoing paths are drawn at equal angles theta to the original direction, one above and one below">
<defs><marker id="s0607ar" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#14181f"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0607gr" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#7b8494"/></marker></defs><line x1="60" y1="150" x2="180" y2="150" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0607ar)"/><text x="120" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">u</text><circle cx="186" cy="150" r="7" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="186" y="180" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">at rest</text><line x1="193" y1="150" x2="340" y2="150" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/><line x1="193" y1="150" x2="304.5" y2="49.6" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-dasharray="6 4" marker-end="url(#s0607gr)"/><line x1="193" y1="150" x2="304.5" y2="250.4" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-dasharray="6 4" marker-end="url(#s0607gr)"/><text x="255" y="124" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">&#952;</text><text x="255" y="180" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">&#952;</text><text x="314" y="44" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">v</text><text x="314" y="256" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">v</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>What is &#952;?</p>`,
  opts: ["&#952; = 45&#176;", "&#952; = 60&#176;", "&#952; = 90&#176;", "&#952; = 30&#176;", "&#952; = 22.5&#176;"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you use both conservation laws and then eliminate the unknown speed between them. Using only one of them leaves an equation with two unknowns, which is where most candidates stop.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — resolve the momentum.</b> Momentum is a vector, so conserving it means conserving each component separately. Take the original direction as the first axis and the perpendicular as the second.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the perpendicular equation.</b> The incoming particle has no perpendicular momentum. The two outgoing particles have equal masses and equal speeds, and they emerge on opposite sides, so their perpendicular momenta are equal and opposite:</p>
<div class="formula">mv sin &#952; &#8722; mv sin &#952; = 0</div>
<p>This is satisfied for any &#952;. It tells us nothing about the angle &#8212; but it does confirm that the symmetry assumed in the question is self-consistent, which is worth knowing before relying on it.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the momentum along the original direction.</b> Each outgoing particle contributes v cos &#952; along the original direction:</p>
<div class="formula">mu = 2mv cos &#952;      so      u = 2v cos &#952;</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the kinetic energy.</b> The collision is elastic, so</p>
<div class="formula">one half m u&#178; = 2 &#215; one half m v&#178;      so      u&#178; = 2v&#178;      and      u = &#8730;2 v</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — divide to eliminate v.</b> Substitute u = &#8730;2 v into the momentum equation:</p>
<div class="formula">&#8730;2 v = 2v cos &#952;      so      cos &#952; = &#8730;2/2</div>
<p>The speed v has cancelled, which is the whole point of using both laws: neither equation alone fixes the angle, but together they do, because the angle appears in one and not the other.</p>
<p><b>Step 6 — the angle.</b> The value &#8730;2/2 is the cosine of 45&#176;, and it is worth knowing by heart, because 45&#176; is the one angle for which the sine and the cosine are equal. So &#952; = 45&#176;.</p>
<p><b>A check.</b> Put the angle back into both laws. With &#952; = 45&#176; the momentum equation gives u = 2v cos 45&#176; = 2v &#215; (&#8730;2/2) = &#8730;2 v, so v = u/&#8730;2. The kinetic energy afterwards is 2 &#215; one half m (u/&#8730;2)&#178; = m u&#178;/2, which is the initial kinetic energy. The momentum along the original direction is 2m &#215; (u/&#8730;2) &#215; (1/&#8730;2) = mu, which is the initial momentum. Both laws are satisfied, so the answer is consistent.</p>
<p>This is the general result for equal masses with one at rest: the two outgoing paths are perpendicular. The symmetry imposed in this question then puts each of them at 45&#176; to the original direction, which is the special case of that general rule.</p>
<p><b>Answer: A</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is to remember that the outgoing paths are perpendicular and then to report 90 degrees as the angle. The perpendicularity is a statement about the SUM of the two angles, not about either one of them, and here the symmetry splits the 90 degrees evenly.`
},

{
  id: "S06-08", module: "E", topic: "Young's modulus: finding a load from a measured extension, using the small-strain assumption", diff: 3, rel: [["E", "Young's modulus is the stress divided by the strain, and the strain is the extension over the ORIGINAL length"], ["E", "The stress is the force divided by the cross-sectional area, and for a small strain the area is unchanged to good approximation"], ["C", "The load is a weight, so the force in newtons is the mass times g"], ["A", "Rearranging a proportionality to isolate the unknown before substituting keeps the arithmetic short"]], key: ["youngsmodulus", "strain", "stress", "smallstrain"], ans: 2, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["E", "Young's modulus is the stress divided by the strain, and the strain is the extension over the ORIGINAL length"], ["E", "The stress is the force divided by the cross-sectional area, and for a small strain the area is unchanged to good approximation"], ["C", "The load is a weight, so the force in newtons is the mass times g"], ["A", "Rearranging a proportionality to isolate the unknown before substituting keeps the arithmetic short"]],
  q: `<p>A steel wire of original length 2.0 m and cross-sectional area 1.0 &#215; 10<sup>&#8722;6</sup> m&#178; hangs vertically. A mass hung from its lower end stretches it by 1.0 mm. The Young modulus of the steel is 2.0 &#215; 10<sup>11</sup> Pa.</p><p>What is the mass?</p>`,
  opts: ["20 kg", "5.0 kg", "10 kg", "100 kg", "2.0 kg"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you write the strain with the original length, and whether you notice how small it is. The smallness is not decoration: it is what makes the answer a single multiplication.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — write the definition in full.</b> Young&#8217;s modulus is</p>
<div class="formula">E = stress / strain = (F/A) / (&#916;&#8467;/&#8467;)</div>
<p>The strain is the extension divided by the <i>original</i> length. Dividing by the stretched length would be wrong, though here the two differ by so little that it would not change the answer &#8212; which is worth knowing, because it means you should not spend time worrying about it.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the strain.</b> Convert the extension to metres first:</p>
<div class="formula">&#916;&#8467; = 1.0 mm = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#179; m
strain = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#179; / 2.0 = 5.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#8308;</div>
<p>A strain of five parts in ten thousand. The wire has stretched by 0.05 % of its length. This is the small-strain regime that the whole theory of Young&#8217;s modulus assumes, and it is why the cross-sectional area may be taken as its original value and the wire&#8217;s own weight negligible beside the load.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the stress.</b> Rearranging the definition:</p>
<div class="formula">stress = E &#215; strain = 2.0 &#215; 10&#185;&#185; &#215; 5.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#8308; = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8312; Pa</div>
<p>That is 100 MPa, which is a sensible working stress for steel &#8212; well below its breaking stress of several hundred MPa, so the wire is in no danger of yielding.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — the force, then the mass.</b></p>
<div class="formula">F = stress &#215; A = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8312; &#215; 1.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8310; = 100 N
m = F/g = 100/10 = 10 kg</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> A 10 kg mass is a bucket of water, and it stretches a one square millimetre steel wire by a millimetre over two metres. That feels right: steel is stiff, and a millimetre is a very small extension for a wire two metres long. If your answer had come out as a tonne, the strain would have had to be a hundred times larger, which the given extension rules out.</p>
<p><b>Answer: C</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the unit of the extension. The strain is a ratio, and a ratio of lengths is dimensionless, so a candidate who leaves the extension in millimetres and the length in metres gets a strain a thousand times too small and a mass of 0.010 kg. Converting to metres before dividing is the whole of the discipline.`
},

{
  id: "S06-09", module: "F", topic: "Superposition of two pulses of opposite polarity, expressed as a fraction of the larger amplitude", diff: 3, rel: [["F", "When two pulses overlap, the resultant displacement is the algebraic sum of the individual displacements"], ["F", "Pulses on a string pass through each other and emerge unchanged, so superposition is instantaneous"], ["A", "A displacement can be positive or negative, and the sign must be carried through the addition"], ["A", "Expressing the answer as a fraction of a named amplitude keeps the arithmetic symbolic"]], key: ["superposition", "pulse", "sign", "amplitude"], ans: 4, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["F", "When two pulses overlap, the resultant displacement is the algebraic sum of the individual displacements"], ["F", "Pulses on a string pass through each other and emerge unchanged, so superposition is instantaneous"], ["A", "A displacement can be positive or negative, and the sign must be carried through the addition"], ["A", "Expressing the answer as a fraction of a named amplitude keeps the arithmetic symbolic"]],
  q: `<p>Two pulses travel towards each other along a stretched string. The pulse from the left is an upward displacement of amplitude 3.0 cm. The pulse from the right is a downward displacement of amplitude 1.0 cm. They meet exactly at the midpoint of the string.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 240" role="img" aria-label="Two rectangular pulses on a string approaching each other. The left-hand pulse is 3.0 centimetres high and positive, the right-hand one is 1.0 centimetre deep and negative, and the midpoint between them is marked with a dashed line">
<defs><marker id="s0609bl" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#2f5fd0"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0609am" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#a8641a"/></marker></defs><line x1="50" y1="150" x2="510" y2="150" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.8"/><line x1="250" y1="70" x2="250" y2="200" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="250" y="216" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">midpoint</text><polyline points="90,150 90,90 170,90 170,150" fill="#e6eefc" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/><polyline points="330,150 330,170 410,170 410,150" fill="#fdf3e0" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/><text x="82" y="96" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#2f5fd0">P</text><text x="130" y="124" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#2f5fd0">+3.0 cm</text><text x="418" y="170" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#a8641a">Q</text><text x="370" y="192" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#a8641a">&#8722;1.0 cm</text><line x1="95" y1="62" x2="150" y2="62" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#s0609bl)"/><line x1="405" y1="62" x2="350" y2="62" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#s0609am)"/><text x="250" y="50" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">each 2.0 m/s</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>Taking the larger amplitude as A, what is the displacement of the string at the midpoint at the instant the two pulses fully overlap?</p>`,
  opts: ["0", "A/3", "A", "4A/3", "2A/3"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you carry the sign of a displacement through the addition. The amplitudes are 3.0 cm and 1.0 cm, and the only thing that decides the answer is that one of them is negative.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — when do they meet?</b> Both pulses travel towards the midpoint, and the figure marks it with a dashed line. Since the pulses are drawn equidistant from it, they arrive together. The question tells you to take the instant of full overlap, so no timing arithmetic is needed.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — assign signs.</b> Take upward as positive. Then the left-hand pulse contributes</p>
<div class="formula">y_1 = +3.0 cm</div>
<p>and the right-hand pulse contributes</p>
<div class="formula">y_2 = &#8722;1.0 cm</div>
<p>Writing the second one down as negative is the whole of the physics. A candidate who writes both as positive is adding two amplitudes that point in opposite directions.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — add them.</b></p>
<div class="formula">y = y_1 + y_2 = 3.0 &#8722; 1.0 = +2.0 cm</div>
<p>The string is 2.0 cm above its rest position at the midpoint. It is not flat, and it is not 4.0 cm up.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — as a fraction of A.</b> The larger amplitude is A = 3.0 cm, so</p>
<div class="formula">y = 2.0/3.0 &#215; A = 2A/3</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — what happens next.</b> The pulses do not bounce off one another. After the instant of overlap each continues in its original direction with its original shape, and a moment later the midpoint is flat again while two separate pulses travel on. Superposition is instantaneous and leaves no trace.</p>
<p>The three wrong answers are the three ways of getting the sign wrong. Reporting zero assumes the pulses are equal and opposite. Reporting A ignores the smaller pulse. Reporting 4A/3 adds the magnitudes, which would be right only if both pulses displaced the string upward.</p>
<p><b>Answer: E</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the sign. The amplitudes 3.0 cm and 1.0 cm look like a simple subtraction problem, and the option list contains the sum 4A/3 as a temptation for anyone who adds the numbers and then has to decide about the sign afterwards.`
},

{
  id: "S06-10", module: "G", topic: "Refraction at a plane surface: finding the speed of light in the material from the two angles, with both measured from the normal", diff: 3, rel: [["G", "Snell&#8217;s law relates the sines of the angles measured from the NORMAL, not the angles from the surface"], ["G", "The refractive index of a material is the speed of light in vacuum divided by the speed in the material"], ["A", "The sine of 60 degrees and the sine of 30 degrees are known exactly, so the ratio is exact"], ["F", "A wave crossing into a slower medium bends towards the normal, which is a statement about the wavefronts"]], key: ["snell", "refractiveindex", "speedoflight", "normal"], ans: 3, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["G", "Snell&#8217;s law relates the sines of the angles measured from the NORMAL, not the angles from the surface"], ["G", "The refractive index of a material is the speed of light in vacuum divided by the speed in the material"], ["A", "The sine of 60 degrees and the sine of 30 degrees are known exactly, so the ratio is exact"], ["F", "A wave crossing into a slower medium bends towards the normal, which is a statement about the wavefronts"]],
  q: `<p>The diagram shows a ray of light travelling in air and meeting the flat surface of a transparent material. The angle of incidence is 60&#176; and the angle of refraction is 30&#176;, both measured from the normal.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 300" role="img" aria-label="A ray of light in air meeting the flat surface of a glass block at 60 degrees to the normal and continuing into the glass at 30 degrees to the normal. The glass has refractive index root three">
<defs><marker id="s0610bl" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#2f5fd0"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0610rd" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#b3352f"/></marker></defs><rect x="60" y="160" width="440" height="110" fill="#eef3fa"/><line x1="60" y1="160" x2="500" y2="160" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.2"/><line x1="260" y1="50" x2="260" y2="275" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><line x1="130.1" y1="85" x2="260" y2="160" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0610bl)"/><line x1="260" y1="160" x2="315" y2="255.3" stroke="#b3352f" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0610rd)"/><polyline points="260,90 255.422,90.1499 250.863,90.5989 246.344,91.345 241.883,92.3852 237.499,93.7149 233.212,95.3284 229.04,97.2189 225,99.3782 221.11,101.797 217.387,104.465 213.846,107.371 210.503,110.503 207.371,113.846 204.465,117.387 201.797,121.11 199.378,125" fill="none" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/><polyline points="260,230 262.29,229.963 264.578,229.85 266.861,229.663 269.137,229.401 271.403,229.065 273.656,228.655 275.895,228.171 278.117,227.615 280.32,226.986 282.501,226.285 284.658,225.513 286.788,224.672 288.889,223.76 290.96,222.781 292.998,221.734 295,220.622" fill="none" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/><text x="212" y="86" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">60&#176;</text><text x="280" y="250" font-size="11.5" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">30&#176;</text><text x="90" y="145" font-size="11" fill="#7b8494">air</text><text x="90" y="190" font-size="11" fill="#7b8494">glass</text><text x="266" y="62" font-size="11" fill="#7b8494">normal</text><text x="490" y="205" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#7b8494">refractive index &#8730;3</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>What is the speed of light inside the material? Take the speed of light in vacuum as 3.0 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>.</p>`,
  opts: ["2.6 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "3.0 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "5.2 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "1.7 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "1.5 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you use the sines rather than the angles, and whether you remember which way the refractive index goes. Both angles in the figure are measured from the normal, which is the convention Snell&#8217;s law needs.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — Snell&#8217;s law.</b> With air taken as having a refractive index of one:</p>
<div class="formula">n = sin i / sin r</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the sines.</b> Both angles are known exactly:</p>
<div class="formula">sin 60&#176; = &#8730;3/2 = 0.866      sin 30&#176; = 1/2 = 0.500</div>
<p>If you measure the angles from the surface instead of from the normal, the roles are exchanged and you would compute 0.500/0.866 = 0.58, which is a refractive index below one and therefore impossible for a material.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the refractive index.</b></p>
<div class="formula">n = (&#8730;3/2) / (1/2) = &#8730;3 = 1.7</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the definition in terms of speed.</b> The refractive index is the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to the speed in the material:</p>
<div class="formula">n = c/v</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — the speed.</b> Rearranging:</p>
<div class="formula">v = c/n = 3.0 &#215; 10&#8312; / 1.7 = 1.7 &#215; 10&#8312; m s&#8315;&#185;</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Light entering a material from air slows down, so the answer must be below 3.0 &#215; 10&#8312; m s&#8315;&#185;. It is, and by a factor of &#8730;3. A speed above that of light in vacuum would be a contradiction, which rules out the 5.2 option without any arithmetic.</p>
<p>Notice the coincidence of the numbers: the refractive index is 1.7 and the speed is 1.7 &#215; 10&#8312; m s&#8315;&#185;. That happens because the speed of light is 3.0 &#215; 10&#8312; and &#8730;3 is close to 1.7. It is worth being able to see that it is a coincidence rather than a rule.</p>
<p><b>Answer: D</b></p>`,
  trap: `Two traps. Measuring the angles from the surface rather than the normal inverts the ratio and gives a refractive index of 0.58, which is physically impossible. And multiplying by the refractive index rather than dividing gives a speed greater than that of light in vacuum, which is the 5.2 option.`
},

{
  id: "S06-11", module: "H", topic: "Power dissipated inside a cell, obtained symbolically by eliminating the current between Ohm's law and the power relation", diff: 2, rel: [["H", "The current in a circuit with internal resistance is the e.m.f. divided by the total resistance"], ["H", "The power dissipated in a resistor is the square of the current times the resistance"], ["A", "Eliminating a quantity between two relations gives an expression in the given symbols alone"], ["A", "Checking that an expression has the dimensions of power rules out the options that do not"]], key: ["internalresistance", "power", "elimination", "symbolic"], ans: 1, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["H", "The current in a circuit with internal resistance is the e.m.f. divided by the total resistance"], ["H", "The power dissipated in a resistor is the square of the current times the resistance"], ["A", "Eliminating a quantity between two relations gives an expression in the given symbols alone"], ["A", "Checking that an expression has the dimensions of power rules out the options that do not"]],
  q: `<p>A cell of e.m.f. &#949; and internal resistance r is connected to an external resistor of resistance R.</p><p>Which expression gives the rate at which energy is dissipated inside the cell?</p>`,
  opts: ["&#949;&#178;r/(R + r)", "&#949;&#178;r/(R + r)&#178;", "&#949;&#178;/(R + r)", "&#949;&#178;R/(R + r)&#178;", "&#949;&#178;r/R&#178;"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can produce an expression rather than a number, and whether you square the current. Every option here is a plausible-looking arrangement of the same four symbols, so recognising one is not enough: it has to be derived.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the current.</b> The internal resistance is in series with the external resistor, so both carry the same current and the total resistance is R + r. Ohm&#8217;s law for the whole circuit gives</p>
<div class="formula">I = &#949;/(R + r)</div>
<p>This is the only current in the circuit. There is no separate current through the cell.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the power in a resistor.</b></p>
<div class="formula">P = I&#178;R_component</div>
<p>The square is not optional. It comes from combining P = VI with V = IR, and dropping it is the single most common error in this question.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — substitute and collect.</b> The component is the internal resistance r:</p>
<div class="formula">P_internal = (&#949;/(R + r))&#178; &#215; r = &#949;&#178;r/(R + r)&#178;</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — check the dimensions.</b> &#949;&#178; has the dimensions of volts squared and (R + r)&#178; has ohms squared, so the ratio is volts squared over ohms. Since V = IR, volts squared over ohms is volts times amperes, which is watts. The expression is a power. The option &#949;&#178;r/(R + r) has an extra ohm in the numerator, which makes it a power times a resistance, so it cannot be right.</p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> If r were zero there would be no energy dissipated inside the cell, and the expression correctly gives zero. If R were zero the cell would be short-circuited, and the expression gives &#949;&#178;/r, which is the largest power the cell can dissipate internally. Both limits are sensible, which is what you want from an expression you cannot evaluate numerically.</p>
<p>For comparison, the power in the external resistor is &#949;&#178;R/(R + r)&#178;, which is the same shape with R in the numerator. That is the fourth option, and it answers a different question.</p>
<p><b>Answer: B</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the missing square. The option without it, &#949;&#178;r/(R + r), has the dimensions of a power multiplied by a resistance, which is not a power at all, so a dimensional check settles it. The other trap is answering for the external resistor, which is the same expression with R in the numerator.`
},

{
  id: "S06-12", module: "K", topic: "The number of nuclei decaying in an interval short compared with the half-life, using the constant-rate approximation", diff: 2, rel: [["K", "The activity of a sample is the decay constant times the number of nuclei present"], ["K", "Over an interval short compared with the half-life the number of nuclei is effectively unchanged, so the activity is constant"], ["K", "The number decaying in an interval is the activity times the interval, which is a rate times a time"], ["A", "Deciding whether an approximation is licensed is part of the work: the half-life must be compared with the interval before the constant-rate shortcut is used"]], key: ["decayconstant", "activity", "shorthalf-life", "rate"], ans: 0, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["K", "The activity of a sample is the decay constant times the number of nuclei present"], ["K", "Over an interval short compared with the half-life the number of nuclei is effectively unchanged, so the activity is constant"], ["K", "The number decaying in an interval is the activity times the interval, which is a rate times a time"], ["A", "Deciding whether an approximation is licensed is part of the work: the half-life must be compared with the interval before the constant-rate shortcut is used"]],
  q: `<p>A sample contains 4.0 &#215; 10<sup>20</sup> radioactive nuclei. The decay constant of the nuclide is 1.0 &#215; 10<sup>&#8722;8</sup> s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>.</p><p>Approximately how many nuclei decay during the first 60 s?</p>`,
  opts: ["2.4 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup>", "4.0 &#215; 10<sup>12</sup>", "2.4 &#215; 10<sup>22</sup>", "2.4 &#215; 10<sup>12</sup>", "2.4 &#215; 10<sup>13</sup>"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The difference between an activity and a count. The activity is a rate per second; the number decaying is that rate multiplied by the time, and only if the rate is effectively constant over the interval.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the activity.</b> The activity is the decay constant times the number of nuclei present:</p>
<div class="formula">A = &#955;N = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8312; &#215; 4.0 &#215; 10&#178;&#8304; = 4.0 &#215; 10&#185;&#178; s&#8315;&#185;</div>
<p>So 4.0 &#215; 10&#185;&#178; nuclei decay every second. That is the rate, not the answer.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — is the rate constant?</b> The half-life is</p>
<div class="formula">t&#189; = ln 2 / &#955; = 0.69 / 1.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8312; &#8776; 6.9 &#215; 10&#8311; s</div>
<p>which is about two years. The interval we are asked about is 60 s, which is smaller than the half-life by a factor of about a million. So the population is unchanged to six significant figures over the interval, and the activity may be treated as constant.</p>
<p>This check is the reason the word "approximately" appears in the question. For an interval comparable with the half-life the constant-rate shortcut would be wrong, and the exponential would have to be used.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the count.</b></p>
<div class="formula">number decaying = A &#215; t = 4.0 &#215; 10&#185;&#178; &#215; 60 = 2.4 &#215; 10&#185;&#8308;</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The number decaying, 2.4 &#215; 10&#185;&#8308;, is 6.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8311; of the 4.0 &#215; 10&#178;&#8304; present. That is a millionth, which is exactly what a 60 s interval out of a two-year half-life should give. If the count had come out comparable with the population, the approximation would have been self-contradictory and the answer would have to be reworked.</p>
<p><b>Answer: A</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is stopping at the activity. 4.0 &#215; 10 to the 12 is the number decaying each second, and it is offered as an option; the question asks for the number over 60 s. The other trap is multiplying without checking that the interval is short compared with the half-life.`
},

{
  id: "S06-13", module: "A", topic: "An estimate of a daily volume: the discriminator is that every conversion from a rate per minute to a total per day must be carried out", diff: 3, rel: [["A", "An estimate is the product of the quantities that make it up, each taken to a convenient round number"], ["A", "Every conversion between the given rate and the asked-for duration must be carried out, and each one is a factor of sixty or twenty-four"], ["A", "Rounding to the nearest power of ten is done at the end and is a decision about the logarithm"], ["A", "The volume of one breath and the number of breaths are independent, so an error in one does not cancel an error in the other"]], key: ["unitconversion", "ratechain", "volume", "dailyrate"], ans: 4, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["A", "An estimate is the product of the quantities that make it up, each taken to a convenient round number"], ["A", "Every conversion between the given rate and the asked-for duration must be carried out, and each one is a factor of sixty or twenty-four"], ["A", "Rounding to the nearest power of ten is done at the end and is a decision about the logarithm"], ["A", "The volume of one breath and the number of breaths are independent, so an error in one does not cancel an error in the other"]],
  q: `<p>A resting adult breathes in about 0.50 litre of air with each breath, at about 12 breaths a minute.</p><p>Estimate, to the nearest power of ten, how many litres of air are breathed in during one day.</p>`,
  opts: ["10<sup>1</sup>", "10<sup>2</sup>", "10<sup>3</sup>", "10<sup>5</sup>", "10<sup>4</sup>"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you carry out every conversion. The volume per breath and the rate are both given, and the only work is turning a rate per minute into a total per day.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what is the answer the product of?</b></p>
<div class="formula">volume = (volume per breath) &#215; (breaths per day)</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the rate, per hour.</b></p>
<div class="formula">12 breaths a minute &#215; 60 minutes an hour = 720 breaths an hour</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the rate, per day.</b></p>
<div class="formula">720 &#215; 24 = 17 280 breaths a day</div>
<p>Stopping here gives 720 &#215; 0.50 = 360 litres. The boundary between 10 to the 2 and 10 to the 3 is &#8730;10 &#215; 10 to the 2, which is about 320, and 360 is above it, so the hourly total alone would already round up to 10 to the 3. That is one of the options, and it is the answer to a question about an hour.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — the volume.</b></p>
<div class="formula">17 280 &#215; 0.50 = 8640 litres</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — round.</b> The boundary between 10 to the 3 and 10 to the 4 is &#8730;10 &#215; 10 to the 3, which is about 3200, and 8640 is well above it, so the answer is 10 to the 4 litres.</p>
<p>A useful sanity check in litres: 8640 litres is 8.6 cubic metres, which is a cube about 2 m on a side. A person breathes out roughly their own volume of air every few minutes, which is the right order of magnitude and is the sort of check that catches a missed factor of sixty.</p>
<p><b>Answer: E</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is stopping one conversion short. Both 10 to the 2 and 10 to the 3 are reachable by doing part of the work, and they are the two most attractive wrong answers precisely because they come from a partial method rather than from a guess.`
},

{
  id: "S06-14", module: "B", topic: "Relative velocity of two bodies moving at right angles, obtained as a vector difference rather than a scalar one", diff: 2, rel: [["B", "Relative velocity is a vector difference, so the directions as well as the magnitudes matter"], ["B", "Two velocities at right angles combine by Pythagoras, because their components along each other&#8217;s directions are independent"], ["A", "Drawing the two velocities nose to tail turns the subtraction into a triangle whose third side is the answer"], ["A", "A right angle between the two given velocities is what makes the arithmetic exact rather than trigonometric"]], key: ["relativevelocity", "vector", "pythagoras", "perpendicular"], ans: 2, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["B", "Relative velocity is a vector difference, so the directions as well as the magnitudes matter"], ["B", "Two velocities at right angles combine by Pythagoras, because their components along each other&#8217;s directions are independent"], ["A", "Drawing the two velocities nose to tail turns the subtraction into a triangle whose third side is the answer"], ["A", "A right angle between the two given velocities is what makes the arithmetic exact rather than trigonometric"]],
  q: `<p>Two cyclists approach a crossroads on straight roads that meet at right angles. Cyclist P travels due east at 15 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>. Cyclist Q travels due north at 20 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 300" role="img" aria-label="A velocity triangle. One arrow points east with magnitude 15 metres per second, one points north with magnitude 20 metres per second, and the third, drawn from the same origin, is their vector difference">
<defs><marker id="s0614bl" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#2f5fd0"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0614gr" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#1f7a53"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0614rd" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#b3352f"/></marker></defs><polygon points="180,150 192,150 192,138 180,138" fill="none" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="180" y1="150" x2="255" y2="150" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0614bl)"/><line x1="180" y1="150" x2="180" y2="50" stroke="#1f7a53" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0614gr)"/><line x1="255" y1="150" x2="255" y2="250" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><line x1="180" y1="150" x2="255" y2="250" stroke="#b3352f" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0614rd)"/><text x="262" y="146" font-size="11.5" font-weight="bold" fill="#2f5fd0">A: 15 m/s east</text><text x="186" y="42" font-size="11.5" font-weight="bold" fill="#1f7a53">B: 20 m/s north</text><text x="262" y="258" font-size="11.5" font-weight="bold" fill="#b3352f">A relative to B</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>What is the speed of P relative to Q?</p>`,
  opts: ["5.0 m/s", "35 m/s", "25 m/s", "18 m/s", "12 m/s"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you treat velocity as a vector. The two speeds, 15 and 20, invite subtraction because that is what "relative" suggests, and subtraction gives 5.0, which is the first option.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the definition.</b> The velocity of P relative to Q is what P&#8217;s velocity looks like to an observer travelling with Q:</p>
<div class="formula">v_PQ = v_P &#8722; v_Q</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — draw the subtraction.</b> Take the two velocity vectors nose to tail, as the figure does. The third side of the triangle, from the tail of the first to the head of the second, is the relative velocity. This is the step that turns an algebraic definition into a picture you can read.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the right angle.</b> P travels east and Q travels north, and the roads meet at right angles, so the two velocity vectors are perpendicular. The triangle is right-angled, and the right-angle marker in the figure is what tells you so.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — Pythagoras.</b></p>
<div class="formula">|v_PQ| = &#8730;(15&#178; + 20&#178;) = &#8730;(225 + 400) = &#8730;625 = 25 m s&#8315;&#185;</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer must lie between the difference of the two speeds and their sum, because that is what a vector difference can do: 20 &#8722; 15 = 5 and 20 + 15 = 35, and 25 is between them. A magnitude outside that range would be impossible. Since the two velocities are perpendicular, the answer is at the upper end of the range, which is where Pythagoras always puts it.</p>
<p>The 15-20-25 triangle is the 3-4-5 triangle scaled by five, which is why the answer is exact and why this question needs no calculator.</p>
<p><b>Answer: C</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is subtracting the magnitudes, which gives 5.0 and is the answer for two bodies moving along the same line in opposite directions. The perpendicularity is stated in the question and drawn in the figure, so both routes to the mistake have been closed off.`
},

{
  id: "S06-15", module: "C", topic: "A two-body system released from rest, where the driving mass reaches the floor and the sliding block is then decelerated by friction alone", diff: 3, rel: [["C", "Two bodies joined by a string have the same acceleration, so they may be treated as one system with the total mass"], ["C", "The tension in the string is internal to the system and cancels when the two equations are added"], ["C", "Friction on a horizontal surface is the coefficient times the normal reaction, and on a horizontal surface the normal reaction is the weight"], ["B", "A body accelerating from rest over a known distance reaches a speed whose square is twice the acceleration times the distance"], ["C", "Once the driving force is removed, the only horizontal force is friction, so the deceleration is the coefficient times g"]], key: ["pulley", "friction", "twostage", "deceleration"], ans: 3, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["C", "Two bodies joined by a string have the same acceleration, so they may be treated as one system with the total mass"], ["C", "The tension in the string is internal to the system and cancels when the two equations are added"], ["C", "Friction on a horizontal surface is the coefficient times the normal reaction, and on a horizontal surface the normal reaction is the weight"], ["B", "A body accelerating from rest over a known distance reaches a speed whose square is twice the acceleration times the distance"], ["C", "Once the driving force is removed, the only horizontal force is friction, so the deceleration is the coefficient times g"]],
  q: `<p>Block A of mass 3.0 kg rests on a rough horizontal table and is joined by a light inextensible string, passing over a frictionless pulley at the table&#8217;s edge, to block B of mass 2.0 kg which hangs freely. The coefficient of friction between A and the table is 0.20, and g is 10 N kg<sup>&#8722;1</sup>.</p><p>The system is released from rest. Block B falls 1.0 m to the floor, and the string then goes slack.</p><p>How much further does block A slide before coming to rest?</p>`,
  opts: ["0.70 m", "2.8 m", "1.0 m", "1.4 m", "0.47 m"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you split the motion into two regimes and carry the speed across the boundary. The system is driven while B falls and undriven afterwards, and a single application of Newton&#8217;s second law cannot cover both.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — while the string is taut, the two blocks are one system.</b> They are joined by an inextensible string, so they have the same acceleration. Treating them together removes the tension, which is internal and pulls in opposite directions on the two blocks.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the driving force.</b> The only force driving the motion is the weight of the hanging block:</p>
<div class="formula">W_B = m_B g = 2.0 &#215; 10 = 20 N</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the retarding force.</b> The block on the table feels friction. On a horizontal table the normal reaction equals its weight:</p>
<div class="formula">N = m_A g = 3.0 &#215; 10 = 30 N
f = &#956;N = 0.20 &#215; 30 = 6.0 N</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the tension cancels.</b> Writing the two equations separately, 20 &#8722; T = 2.0a for B and T &#8722; 6.0 = 3.0a for A, and adding them gives 20 &#8722; 6.0 = 5.0a. The tension has disappeared, which is why treating the two as a system is not a shortcut but the same algebra done in one line.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — the acceleration.</b></p>
<div class="formula">a = (20 &#8722; 6.0)/5.0 = 14/5.0 = 2.8 m s&#8315;&#178;</div>
<p><b>Step 6 — the speed when B lands.</b> B falls 1.0 m, and A moves 1.0 m in the same time because the string does not stretch. Starting from rest:</p>
<div class="formula">v&#178; = 2as = 2 &#215; 2.8 &#215; 1.0 = 5.6</div>
<p><b>Step 7 — the second regime.</b> B has landed, so the string is slack and no longer pulls A. The only horizontal force on A is friction, acting backwards.</p>
<p><b>Step 8 — the deceleration.</b> Newton&#8217;s second law for A alone:</p>
<div class="formula">a&#8242; = f/m_A = 6.0/3.0 = 2.0 m s&#8315;&#178;</div>
<p>Equivalently, a&#8242; = &#956;g = 0.20 &#215; 10 = 2.0 m s&#8315;&#178;. The mass cancels, which is why the stopping distance of a sliding object does not depend on how heavy it is.</p>
<p><b>Step 9 — the stopping distance.</b> Using v&#178; = 2a&#8242;s again, this time with the final speed zero:</p>
<div class="formula">s = v&#178;/(2a&#8242;) = 5.6/(2 &#215; 2.0) = 5.6/4.0 = 1.4 m</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The deceleration, 2.0 m s&#8315;&#178;, is smaller than the acceleration, 2.8 m s&#8315;&#178;, so the second stage must be longer than the first: A accelerated over 1.0 m and coasts to a stop over 1.4 m. If the deceleration had come out larger than the acceleration, the stopping distance would have had to be shorter than 1.0 m, and an answer above it would have been wrong on that ground alone.</p>
<p>Note also that the stopping distance is the answer to the question as asked. The total distance A travels is 2.4 m, which is not asked for; the 1.0 m it travels while B is still falling is behind it by the time the string goes slack.</p>
<p><b>Answer: D</b></p>`,
  trap: `Two traps. The first is using the whole 2.4 m of travel in the second stage, which double-counts the driven part. The second is forgetting that friction continues after the string goes slack: a candidate who assumes A stops as soon as the drive is removed reports no further travel at all, or divides by the wrong acceleration.`
},

{
  id: "S06-16", module: "D", topic: "The greatest speed round a flat bend, obtained by equating the friction limit to the centripetal force and giving the answer as an expression", diff: 3, rel: [["D", "A body moving in a circle accelerates towards the centre, and the force producing that acceleration is the centripetal force"], ["D", "On a flat horizontal bend the only horizontal force available is friction, and the friction points towards the centre"], ["C", "The friction available is the coefficient times the normal reaction, and on a flat road the normal reaction is the weight"], ["A", "The mass appears on both sides of the equation and cancels, so the answer does not depend on it"]], key: ["circularmotion", "friction", "centripetal", "symbolic"], ans: 0, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["D", "A body moving in a circle accelerates towards the centre, and the force producing that acceleration is the centripetal force"], ["D", "On a flat horizontal bend the only horizontal force available is friction, and the friction points towards the centre"], ["C", "The friction available is the coefficient times the normal reaction, and on a flat road the normal reaction is the weight"], ["A", "The mass appears on both sides of the equation and cancels, so the answer does not depend on it"]],
  q: `<p>A car of mass m goes round a flat circular bend of radius r. The coefficient of friction between the tyres and the road is &#956;.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 300" role="img" aria-label="A plan view of a car on a circular track of radius R. The friction force on the car is drawn along the radius, pointing towards the centre of the circle">
<defs><marker id="s0616gr" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#1f7a53"/></marker></defs><circle cx="280" cy="150" r="100" fill="none" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="280" cy="150" r="4" fill="#14181f" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="0"/><rect x="266" y="42" width="28" height="14" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="280" y="34" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">car</text><line x1="280" y1="110" x2="280" y2="150" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="272" y="134" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#7b8494">R</text><line x1="280" y1="58" x2="280" y2="100" stroke="#1f7a53" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0616gr)"/><text x="288" y="84" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#1f7a53">f</text><text x="280" y="272" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">track, radius R</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>Which expression gives the greatest speed at which the car can take the bend without sliding?</p>`,
  opts: ["&#8730;(&#956;gr)", "&#956;gr", "&#8730;(2&#956;gr)", "&#8730;(&#956;gr/2)", "2&#8730;(&#956;gr)"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know which force supplies the centripetal force on a flat bend, and whether you can cancel the mass before trying to solve for the speed. The answer is an expression, so the work is algebra rather than arithmetic.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what is pushing the car towards the centre?</b> The car is on a flat road, so there is no component of the weight towards the centre and no banked surface to help. The only horizontal force is friction between the tyres and the road, and it must point radially inward. That is what the red arrow in the figure shows. A candidate who draws the friction along the direction of travel has no centripetal force at all.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the force needed.</b> For motion in a circle of radius r at speed v:</p>
<div class="formula">F = m v&#178;/r</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the force available.</b> The friction has a maximum, reached when the tyres are on the point of sliding:</p>
<div class="formula">f_max = &#956;N = &#956;mg</div>
<p>On a flat road the normal reaction is the weight. On a banked road it would not be, which is why this question specifies flat.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — equate and cancel.</b></p>
<div class="formula">m v&#178;/r = &#956;mg</div>
<p>The mass appears on both sides and cancels. This is worth pausing on: it means the answer does not depend on whether the vehicle is a bicycle or a lorry. A heavier vehicle needs a larger centripetal force, and it also has a proportionally larger friction limit, so the two effects cancel exactly.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — solve for the speed.</b></p>
<div class="formula">v&#178; = &#956;gr      so      v = &#8730;(&#956;gr)</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The speed rises with the square root of the radius, so a bend of four times the radius can be taken at twice the speed. It rises with the square root of the coefficient, so doubling the grip multiplies the speed by 1.4 rather than by two. Both of those are things one can check by driving, and both are consistent with everyday experience.</p>
<p>With the numbers of a typical bend, &#956; = 0.60, r = 50 m and g = 10 N kg&#8315;&#185;, the speed is &#8730;300 = 17 m s&#8315;&#185;, which is about 60 km per hour. That is the right order for a wet roundabout, which is a further check on the expression.</p>
<p><b>Answer: A</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the mass. It looks as though the answer must depend on m, and an option that keeps m would be tempting; but the mass cancels, and the fact that it does is the physical content of the question. The second trap is forgetting the square root, which leaves &#956;gr, the square of the speed.`
},

{
  id: "S06-17", module: "E", topic: "Linear thermal expansion of a rail, using the original length and a temperature difference in kelvin", diff: 3, rel: [["E", "The increase in length is the original length times the coefficient times the temperature rise"], ["E", "The coefficient is defined per kelvin, and a temperature difference is the same number of kelvin as of degrees Celsius"], ["A", "A product of three quantities, two of them small, is best done by collecting the powers of ten before multiplying"], ["J", "The same temperature difference drives every thermal expansion, so the kelvin and the degree Celsius scales are interchangeable for a difference"]], key: ["expansion", "coefficient", "temperaturedifference", "powersoften"], ans: 4, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["E", "The increase in length is the original length times the coefficient times the temperature rise"], ["E", "The coefficient is defined per kelvin, and a temperature difference is the same number of kelvin as of degrees Celsius"], ["A", "A product of three quantities, two of them small, is best done by collecting the powers of ten before multiplying"], ["J", "The same temperature difference drives every thermal expansion, so the kelvin and the degree Celsius scales are interchangeable for a difference"]],
  q: `<p>A steel rail is 2.0 m long at 20 &#176;C. The linear expansivity of steel is 1.2 &#215; 10<sup>&#8722;5</sup> K<sup>&#8722;1</sup>.</p><p>What is the increase in the rail&#8217;s length when it is heated to 120 &#176;C?</p>`,
  opts: ["0.24 mm", "24 mm", "1.2 mm", "4.8 mm", "2.4 mm"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you take the temperature DIFFERENCE rather than the final temperature, and whether you keep track of the powers of ten. The arithmetic is a single multiplication.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the relation.</b> The increase in length is</p>
<div class="formula">&#916;&#8467; = &#8467;&#8320; &#945; &#916;T</div>
<p>The length is the <i>original</i> length, not the expanded one. The difference matters at the level of a thousandth of a per cent here, but writing the relation correctly is what makes it not matter at all.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the temperature rise.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#916;T = 120 &#8722; 20 = 100 K</div>
<p>The coefficient is quoted per kelvin, and a temperature <i>difference</i> of 100 degrees Celsius is a difference of 100 kelvin. This is the one place where the two scales can be mixed without error, and it is worth knowing exactly why: the offsets cancel when you subtract. Using the final temperature, 120 &#176;C, as the rise would be the mistake that gives 4.8 mm.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the expansion per kelvin.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#8467;&#8320;&#945; = 2.0 &#215; 1.2 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; = 2.4 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; m per kelvin</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the expansion.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#916;&#8467; = 2.4 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; &#215; 100 = 2.4 &#215; 10&#8315;&#179; m = 2.4 mm</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The rail has grown by 2.4 mm in 2.0 m, which is about one part in a thousand. This is the check that matters, because the whole relation &#916;&#8467; = &#8467;&#8320;&#945;&#916;T assumes the coefficient is constant over the range and that the expansion is a small perturbation. Both are true here. If the answer had come out as centimetres, the linear relation itself would have been in doubt.</p>
<p>Two millimetres on a two metre rail sounds negligible until you consider that rails are joined end to end: over a kilometre of track the same expansion amounts to more than a metre, which is why real track is laid with expansion gaps.</p>
<p><b>Answer: E</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is using the final temperature rather than the rise. The question gives two temperatures, 20 and 120, and a candidate who reads the 120 as the rise gets exactly twice the answer. The other trap is the powers of ten: the coefficient is 10 to the minus 5 and the expansion is wanted in millimetres, which is 10 to the minus 3, so two conversions must be kept straight.`
},

{
  id: "S06-18", module: "F", topic: "The Doppler shift for a source receding from a stationary observer, expanded to first order in the ratio of the speeds", diff: 2, rel: [["F", "A source moving away from an observer stretches the wavelength ahead of it, so the observed frequency falls"], ["F", "The exact relation for a receding source is the source frequency divided by one plus the ratio of the speeds"], ["A", "For a small ratio the reciprocal may be expanded to first order, giving one minus the ratio"], ["A", "Checking the sign of a correction against the physics is faster than re-deriving it"]], key: ["doppler", "receding", "firstorder", "sign"], ans: 1, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["F", "A source moving away from an observer stretches the wavelength ahead of it, so the observed frequency falls"], ["F", "The exact relation for a receding source is the source frequency divided by one plus the ratio of the speeds"], ["A", "For a small ratio the reciprocal may be expanded to first order, giving one minus the ratio"], ["A", "Checking the sign of a correction against the physics is faster than re-deriving it"]],
  q: `<p>A source of sound of frequency f moves directly away from a stationary observer at a steady speed v. The speed of sound in air is u, and v is much smaller than u.</p><p>Which expression gives the frequency heard by the observer, to first order in v/u?</p>`,
  opts: ["f(1 + v/u)", "f(1 &#8722; v/u)", "fu/(u &#8722; v)", "f(1 &#8722; u/v)", "f(1 + v&#178;/u&#178;)"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know the sign of the Doppler correction, and whether you can expand a reciprocal to first order. The phrase "to first order in v/u" tells you the expansion is expected.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what must the answer do?</b> The source is moving away. Each wavefront is emitted from a point slightly further from the observer than the last, so the waves arrive stretched out and the observed frequency is lower than the emitted one. Whatever expression you choose, it must give a frequency below f.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the exact relation.</b> For a source receding at speed v in a medium where sound travels at u:</p>
<div class="formula">f&#8242; = f u/(u + v)</div>
<p>Dividing the top and bottom by u puts it in terms of the single small ratio:</p>
<div class="formula">f&#8242; = f / (1 + v/u)</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — expand.</b> For a small quantity x, 1/(1 + x) is approximately 1 &#8722; x. Here x = v/u, which the question states is small, so</p>
<div class="formula">f&#8242; &#8776; f(1 &#8722; v/u)</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The factor is below one, so the observed frequency is below the emitted one, which is what a receding source must give. As a further check, if v were zero the factor would be exactly one and the observed frequency would equal the emitted one, which is right.</p>
<p>The expansion also shows why the phrase "to first order" matters. The exact factor is 1 &#8722; x + x&#178; &#8722; &#8230;, so the next term is a hundredth of a per cent for a ratio of one per cent. For sound from a car the ratio is of order 0.03, and the second-order term is a thousandth of the first, which is why the linear form is the one worth remembering.</p>
<p><b>Why the other options fail.</b> f(1 + v/u) and fu/(u &#8722; v) both raise the frequency, so both describe an approaching source. f(1 &#8722; u/v) inverts the ratio: when v is small, u/v is large, so the correction would be enormous, which contradicts the assumption the question makes. And f(1 + v&#178;/u&#178;) is second order, so it misses the leading term entirely.</p>
<p><b>Answer: B</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the sign. A candidate who remembers only that the Doppler effect changes the frequency, without remembering which way, is choosing between five options that differ mainly in sign. The physical check &#8212; a receding source is heard lower &#8212; settles it before any algebra is done.`
},

{
  id: "S06-19", module: "G", topic: "Apparent depth of an object seen through a transparent liquid, found by dividing the real depth by the refractive index", diff: 2, rel: [["G", "Light from an object under a surface refracts away from the normal on leaving the liquid, so the rays appear to come from a shallower point"], ["G", "The apparent depth is the real depth divided by the refractive index of the liquid"], ["G", "The image is on the same side of the surface as the object and above it, never below"], ["A", "Checking that a result is smaller than the quantity it came from is a one-step check on the direction of the relation"]], key: ["apparentdepth", "refraction", "image", "index"], ans: 2, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["G", "Light from an object under a surface refracts away from the normal on leaving the liquid, so the rays appear to come from a shallower point"], ["G", "The apparent depth is the real depth divided by the refractive index of the liquid"], ["G", "The image is on the same side of the surface as the object and above it, never below"], ["A", "Checking that a result is smaller than the quantity it came from is a one-step check on the direction of the relation"]],
  q: `<p>A small stone lies on the bottom of a tank of liquid of uniform depth 3.0 m. The refractive index of the liquid is 1.5.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 300" role="img" aria-label="An object at the bottom of a pool, a ray travelling up to the surface and refracting away from the normal, and the backward extension of that ray meeting the vertical sight line at the apparent image, which is above the real object">
<defs><marker id="s0619am" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#a8641a"/></marker></defs><defs><marker id="s0619rd" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#b3352f"/></marker></defs><rect x="60" y="120" width="440" height="160" fill="#e8f1fa"/><line x1="60" y1="120" x2="500" y2="120" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.2"/><line x1="290" y1="40" x2="290" y2="180" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><line x1="250" y1="260" x2="250" y2="50" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="3 5"/><line x1="250" y1="260" x2="290" y2="120" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0619am)"/><line x1="290" y1="120" x2="321.5" y2="40" stroke="#b3352f" stroke-width="2.2" marker-end="url(#s0619rd)"/><line x1="290" y1="120" x2="250" y2="221.5" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><circle cx="250" cy="260" r="5.5" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.8"/><circle cx="250" cy="221.5" r="5.5" fill="none" stroke="#b3352f" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="258" y="266" font-size="11" fill="#14181f">object</text><text x="258" y="226" font-size="11" fill="#b3352f">apparent image</text><text x="95" y="100" font-size="11" fill="#7b8494">air</text><text x="95" y="150" font-size="11" fill="#7b8494">water</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>What is the apparent depth of the stone when it is viewed from directly above?</p>`,
  opts: ["4.5 m", "1.5 m", "2.0 m", "0.67 m", "3.0 m"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The direction of the relation. Apparent depth is the real depth divided by the refractive index, so the bottom of a pool always looks closer than it is. Getting the division the wrong way round gives a tank that looks half as deep again, which is the first option.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — why the image moves.</b> A ray from the stone travels up through the liquid and bends away from the normal as it enters the air, because it is passing into a medium in which light travels faster. The eye does not know the ray has bent; it traces the ray backwards in a straight line. That backwards line meets the vertical through the stone at a point above the stone. That point is where the stone appears to be.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the relation.</b> For small angles, so that the sines may be replaced by the tangents, the geometry gives</p>
<div class="formula">apparent depth = real depth / n</div>
<p>The index divides. This is worth remembering as a physical statement rather than a formula: a pool looks shallower than it is, so the apparent depth must be the smaller number, and the only way to get a smaller number from a depth and an index greater than one is to divide.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — substitute.</b></p>
<div class="formula">apparent depth = 3.0/1.5 = 2.0 m</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer is two thirds of the real depth, which is a substantial shift: the stone appears a full metre higher than it is. That is consistent with everyday experience of looking into water, and it is why spearfishing is difficult. The answer is also smaller than the real depth, as it must be.</p>
<p>The figure shows the apparent image between the object and the surface, which is the geometric statement of the same thing. If your working put the image below the stone, the figure tells you immediately that something has been inverted.</p>
<p><b>Answer: C</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is multiplying instead of dividing, which gives 4.5 m and describes a pool that looks deeper than it is. The physical check &#8212; water always looks shallower than it is &#8212; rules that out before any formula is used.`
},

{
  id: "S06-20", module: "H", topic: "A series resistor in front of a parallel pair: finding the current in one branch of the pair", diff: 3, rel: [["H", "Resistors in parallel combine to less than either, and the combination is what the series resistor sees"], ["H", "The current from the cell is the e.m.f. divided by the total resistance"], ["H", "The p.d. across the parallel pair is the current times the pair&#8217;s resistance, not the whole supply voltage"], ["H", "The currents in the two branches of a parallel pair divide in the INVERSE ratio of their resistances, so the smaller resistance carries the larger current"]], key: ["parallelpair", "series", "branchcurrent", "divider"], ans: 3, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["H", "Resistors in parallel combine to less than either, and the combination is what the series resistor sees"], ["H", "The current from the cell is the e.m.f. divided by the total resistance"], ["H", "The p.d. across the parallel pair is the current times the pair&#8217;s resistance, not the whole supply voltage"], ["H", "The currents in the two branches of a parallel pair divide in the INVERSE ratio of their resistances, so the smaller resistance carries the larger current"]],
  q: `<p>The cell has an e.m.f. of 6.0 V and negligible internal resistance. It is connected to a 3.0 &#937; resistor in series with a parallel pair made of a 6.0 &#937; and a 2.0 &#937; resistor, as the diagram shows.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 290" role="img" aria-label="A 6.0 volt cell connected to a 3.0 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair made of a 6.0 ohm and a 2.0 ohm resistor">
<line x1="80" y1="80" x2="420" y2="80" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="80" y1="220" x2="420" y2="220" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="80" y1="80" x2="80" y2="143" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="80" y1="157" x2="80" y2="220" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="66" y1="143" x2="94" y2="143" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="74" y1="157" x2="86" y2="157" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="4.5"/><text x="100" y="154" font-size="11.5" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">6.0 V</text><rect x="150" y="68" width="80" height="24" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="190" y="60" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">3.0 &#937;</text><line x1="320" y1="80" x2="320" y2="128" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="308" y="128" width="24" height="64" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="320" y1="192" x2="320" y2="220" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><text x="300" y="164" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">6.0 &#937;</text><line x1="420" y1="80" x2="420" y2="128" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="408" y="128" width="24" height="64" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="420" y1="192" x2="420" y2="220" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><text x="400" y="164" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="end" font-weight="bold" fill="#14181f">2.0 &#937;</text><circle cx="320" cy="80" r="4" fill="#14181f" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="0"/><circle cx="320" cy="220" r="4" fill="#14181f" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="0"/>
</svg>
</figure><p>What is the current in the 2.0 &#937; resistor?</p>`,
  opts: ["1.3 A", "2.0 A", "0.67 A", "1.0 A", "0.33 A"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you find the p.d. across the parallel pair before dividing by a branch resistance. Using the full 6.0 V across the 2.0 &#937; gives 3.0 A, which is far too large; using the cell current as the branch current gives 1.3 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — combine the pair.</b></p>
<div class="formula">R_pair = 6.0 &#215; 2.0/(6.0 + 2.0) = 12/8 = 1.5 &#937;</div>
<p>The pair is smaller than either of its members, which is a check worth making every time.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the circuit resistance.</b> The pair is in series with the 3.0 &#937;:</p>
<div class="formula">R = 3.0 + 1.5 = 4.5 &#937;</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the current from the cell.</b> The internal resistance is negligible here, so the whole 6.0 V appears across the external circuit:</p>
<div class="formula">I = 6.0/4.5 = 1.3 A</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the p.d. across the pair.</b> This is the step that decides the question. The pair does not sit across the full 6.0 V, because the 3.0 &#937; takes 1.3 &#215; 3.0 = 4.0 V of it first:</p>
<div class="formula">V_pair = 1.3 &#215; 1.5 = 2.0 V</div>
<p>As a check, 4.0 V + 2.0 V = 6.0 V, which is the whole supply. The p.d.s in a series circuit must add to the supply voltage, and here they do.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — the branch current.</b></p>
<div class="formula">I(2.0 &#937;) = 2.0/2.0 = 1.0 A</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The other branch carries 2.0/6.0 = 0.33 A, and 1.0 + 0.33 = 1.3 A, which is the current from the cell. Every junction must balance, and this one does.</p>
<p>Notice which branch carries more. The 2.0 &#937; carries three times the current of the 6.0 &#937;, because the currents in a parallel pair divide in the inverse ratio of the resistances. A candidate who splits the 1.3 A in the ratio 6 : 2 instead of 2 : 6 gets 0.33 A and 1.0 A the wrong way round, which is exactly the last option.</p>
<p><b>Answer: D</b></p>`,
  trap: `Two traps. The first is using the supply voltage across the pair, which gives 3.0 A. The second is splitting the cell current in the ratio of the resistances rather than the inverse ratio, which gives the smaller share to the smaller resistance. The figure marks the junctions so that which pair is in parallel is unambiguous.`
},

{
  id: "S06-21", module: "I", topic: "Charge sharing between two capacitors: charge is conserved but energy is not, so a fraction of the energy is dissipated", diff: 2, rel: [["I", "The charge on a capacitor is its capacitance times the potential difference across it"], ["I", "Capacitors in parallel add, so the total capacitance after connection is the sum"], ["I", "Charge is conserved when a charged capacitor is connected to an uncharged one, because no charge leaves the circuit"], ["I", "The energy stored in a capacitor is one half of the charge times the potential difference, or one half C V squared"], ["A", "Energy need not be conserved in a process that is not elastic, and charge sharing is such a process"]], key: ["capacitor", "chargesharing", "energy", "conservation"], ans: 0, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["I", "The charge on a capacitor is its capacitance times the potential difference across it"], ["I", "Capacitors in parallel add, so the total capacitance after connection is the sum"], ["I", "Charge is conserved when a charged capacitor is connected to an uncharged one, because no charge leaves the circuit"], ["I", "The energy stored in a capacitor is one half of the charge times the potential difference, or one half C V squared"], ["A", "Energy need not be conserved in a process that is not elastic, and charge sharing is such a process"]],
  q: `<p>A 4.0 &#956;F capacitor is charged to a potential difference of 10 V and then disconnected from the supply. It is connected directly across an uncharged 6.0 &#956;F capacitor.</p><p>What fraction of the original stored energy is dissipated in the connecting wires?</p>`,
  opts: ["60 %", "40 %", "50 %", "0 %", "20 %"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know which quantity is conserved. Charge is; energy is not. A candidate who assumes both are conserved reaches the fourth option and has to believe that no energy was lost, which the resistance of the connecting wires rules out.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what is conserved?</b> The two capacitors are disconnected from the supply and joined to each other, so the charge has nowhere to go except from one plate to the other. The total charge is therefore unchanged. The energy is not: charge moves through wires that have resistance, and the current that flows heats them.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the charge before.</b></p>
<div class="formula">Q = C&#8321;V&#8321; = 4.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8310; &#215; 10 = 4.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; C</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the common potential.</b> After connection the two capacitors are in parallel and therefore share the same potential. Their capacitances add:</p>
<div class="formula">C_total = 4.0 + 6.0 = 10 &#956;F
V = Q/C_total = 4.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; / 10 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8310; = 4.0 V</div>
<p>The common potential is below the original 10 V, which is what you expect when the charge is spread over a larger capacitance.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — the energy before and after.</b></p>
<div class="formula">E_before = &#189; C&#8321;V&#8321;&#178; = &#189; &#215; 4.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8310; &#215; 100 = 2.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8308; J
E_after = &#189; C_total V&#178; = &#189; &#215; 10 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8310; &#215; 16 = 8.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; J</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — the fraction lost.</b></p>
<div class="formula">E_lost = 2.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8308; &#8722; 8.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; = 1.2 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8308; J
fraction = 1.2/2.0 = 0.60</div>
<p>So 60 % of the stored energy is dissipated. Note that the total charge afterwards is 10 &#956;F &#215; 4.0 V = 4.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8309; C, which is exactly the charge we started with, so the conservation of charge is confirmed.</p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer must lie between zero and one, and it does. It also cannot be zero, because the final potential is 4.0 V rather than 10 V and the energy stored falls with the square of the potential: had the energy been conserved, the final potential would have had to be higher than the initial one, which the charge calculation rules out.</p>
<p>It is worth noticing that the fraction lost does not depend on the initial voltage. Connecting a 4.0 &#956;F to a 6.0 &#956;F always loses 60 % of the energy, whatever the starting potential, because both the before and the after energy scale with the square of it.</p>
<p><b>Answer: A</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is assuming energy is conserved. Charge sharing is not an elastic process: the current that redistributes the charge dissipates energy in the resistance of the wires, and there is no way to connect two capacitors without it. The 0 % option is the misconception stated plainly.`
},

{
  id: "S06-22", module: "J", topic: "Specific latent heat of fusion from the length of the plateau on a heating curve at constant power", diff: 2, rel: [["J", "During a change of state the temperature stays constant while energy is supplied, so a plateau on a heating curve means latent heat"], ["J", "The energy supplied at constant power is the power times the time, so the lengths of the stages on the graph are proportional to the energies"], ["J", "The specific latent heat is the energy needed to melt one kilogram at the melting point"], ["A", "A ratio of two times on the same graph is a ratio of two energies, so the power cancels if it is not given"]], key: ["latentheat", "plateau", "heatingcurve", "power"], ans: 2, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["J", "During a change of state the temperature stays constant while energy is supplied, so a plateau on a heating curve means latent heat"], ["J", "The energy supplied at constant power is the power times the time, so the lengths of the stages on the graph are proportional to the energies"], ["J", "The specific latent heat is the energy needed to melt one kilogram at the melting point"], ["A", "A ratio of two times on the same graph is a ratio of two energies, so the power cancels if it is not given"]],
  q: `<p>A 0.30 kg sample of a solid is heated at a constant rate of 150 W. Its temperature rises from 20 &#176;C to 80 &#176;C in 4.0 min, then stays at 80 &#176;C for the next 6.0 min while the sample melts, and then rises again, as the graph shows.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 322" role="img" aria-label="A heating curve. The temperature of a solid rises from 20 to 80 degrees Celsius in 4.0 minutes, stays at 80 for 6.0 minutes while the solid melts, and then rises again">
<defs><marker id="s0622ar" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3.2" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.2 L0,6.4 z" fill="#14181f"/></marker></defs><polygon points="206.7,90 396.7,90 396.7,112 206.7,112" fill="#fdf3e0" stroke="none" stroke-width="0" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="80" y1="250" x2="510" y2="250" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0622ar)"/><line x1="80" y1="250" x2="80" y2="50" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#s0622ar)"/><polyline points="80,210 206.7,90 396.7,90 460,70" fill="none" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/><text x="80" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">0</text><text x="206.7" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">4.0</text><text x="396.7" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">10</text><text x="460" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#14181f">12</text><text x="72" y="214" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#14181f">20</text><text x="72" y="94" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#14181f">80</text><text x="86" y="44" font-size="11" fill="#14181f">temperature / &#176;C</text><text x="540" y="288" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#14181f">time / min</text><text x="130" y="236" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">solid</text><text x="301" y="80" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#a8641a">melting</text><text x="434" y="152" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">liquid</text><line x1="206.7" y1="290" x2="396.7" y2="290" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="206.7" y1="285" x2="206.7" y2="295" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="396.7" y1="285" x2="396.7" y2="295" stroke="#a8641a" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="301" y="310" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#a8641a">6.0 min</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>What is the specific latent heat of fusion of the sample?</p>`,
  opts: ["1.2 &#215; 10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "3.0 &#215; 10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "1.8 &#215; 10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "9.0 &#215; 10<sup>4</sup> J kg<sup>&#8722;1</sup>", "3.6 &#215; 10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>&#8722;1</sup>"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know what a plateau means, and whether you use the right time from the graph. The plateau is the melting stage; the 4.0 min before it is the warming stage, and using that time instead is the first option.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — read the graph.</b> The temperature rises from 20 &#176;C to 80 &#176;C over the first 4.0 min. It then stays at 80 &#176;C from 4.0 min to 10 min, which is a plateau 6.0 min long. Then it rises again. A constant temperature while energy is being supplied means the energy is being used to change the state, not to raise the temperature. That is the definition of a latent heat.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the plateau&#8217;s length in seconds.</b></p>
<div class="formula">t = 6.0 min = 6.0 &#215; 60 = 360 s</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the energy supplied during melting.</b> The power is constant, so</p>
<div class="formula">E = P t = 150 &#215; 360 = 54 000 J</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the specific latent heat.</b> The specific latent heat of fusion is the energy needed to melt one kilogram at the melting point:</p>
<div class="formula">L = E/m = 54 000/0.30 = 180 000 J kg&#8315;&#185;</div>
<p>That is 1.8 &#215; 10&#8309; J kg&#8315;&#185;.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — the check the graph gives you for free.</b> The warming stage supplies 150 &#215; 240 = 36 000 J, and the plateau supplies 54 000 J. The plateau is half again as long as the warming stage, and it supplies half again as much energy. The two facts agree, which they must because the power is constant. If your answer had used the 4.0 min you would have got 1.2 &#215; 10&#8309;, which is the warming stage&#8217;s energy per kilogram rather than the latent heat.</p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The specific latent heat of fusion of ice is 3.3 &#215; 10&#8309; J kg&#8315;&#185;, and of lead about 2.3 &#215; 10&#8308;. The value here, 1.8 &#215; 10&#8309;, sits between them, which is the right region for a solid with moderate intermolecular forces. A value of 10&#8311; would be a hundred times ice and would signal an arithmetic slip.</p>
<p><b>Answer: C</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is using the warming stage's time for the melting stage. Both stages are on the same graph and both are labelled, so the only defence is to know that the plateau is the one that carries the latent heat. A second trap is forgetting to convert minutes to seconds, which gives an answer sixty times too small.`
},

{
  id: "S06-23", module: "K", topic: "The fraction of a radioactive sample remaining after a whole number of half-lives", diff: 1, rel: [["K", "After each half-life the number of undecayed nuclei is halved"], ["K", "The number of half-lives is the elapsed time divided by the half-life"], ["A", "Repeated halving is a power of one half, not a division by the number of steps"]], key: ["halflife", "fraction", "repeatedhalving"], ans: 1, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["K", "After each half-life the number of undecayed nuclei is halved"], ["K", "The number of half-lives is the elapsed time divided by the half-life"], ["A", "Repeated halving is a power of one half, not a division by the number of steps"]],
  q: `<p>A radioisotope has a half-life of 8.0 days.</p><p>What fraction of the nuclei present initially remain after 24 days?</p>`,
  opts: ["1/3", "1/8", "1/6", "1/24", "1/16"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you halve repeatedly rather than dividing. After one half-life half remain; after two, a quarter; after three, an eighth.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — how many half-lives?</b></p>
<div class="formula">24 days / 8.0 days per half-life = 3 half-lives</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — halve three times.</b></p>
<div class="formula">1 &#8594; 1/2 &#8594; 1/4 &#8594; 1/8</div>
<p>The fraction remaining is 1/8.</p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer must be a power of one half, because each half-life multiplies the surviving fraction by one half. It also must not be zero: after three half-lives there is still an eighth of the sample, and after ten there is still a thousandth. Decay never quite finishes, which is why the question asks for a fraction rather than for "how many are left".</p>
<p><b>Answer: B</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is dividing 1 by 3 rather than raising one half to the third power. The two operations differ because decay is exponential: the loss in each half-life is half of what is left at the time, not half of the original sample.`
},

{
  id: "S06-24", module: "L", topic: "Threshold wavelength for the photoelectric effect, obtained by equating the photon energy to the work function", diff: 2, rel: [["L", "A photon of frequency f carries energy hf, and the energy may also be written in terms of its wavelength as hc over the wavelength"], ["L", "The work function is the least energy needed to remove an electron from the surface"], ["L", "The threshold is the case where the photon energy exactly equals the work function, leaving the electron with no kinetic energy"], ["A", "Dimensional reasoning identifies which arrangement of h, c and the work function has the dimensions of a length"]], key: ["photoelectric", "threshold", "workfunction", "dimensional"], ans: 4, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["L", "A photon of frequency f carries energy hf, and the energy may also be written in terms of its wavelength as hc over the wavelength"], ["L", "The work function is the least energy needed to remove an electron from the surface"], ["L", "The threshold is the case where the photon energy exactly equals the work function, leaving the electron with no kinetic energy"], ["A", "Dimensional reasoning identifies which arrangement of h, c and the work function has the dimensions of a length"]],
  q: `<p>A metal surface has a work function &#966;. Light of wavelength &#955; falls on it and releases electrons.</p><p>Which expression gives the longest wavelength of light that will just release an electron from this surface?</p>`,
  opts: ["&#966;/(hc)", "hc&#966;", "h/(c&#966;)", "c&#966;/h", "hc/&#966;"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can set up the threshold condition and rearrange it, and whether you can check the result by dimensions when the answer is an expression rather than a number.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the photon energy in terms of wavelength.</b> A photon of frequency f carries energy hf, and since c = f&#955; the frequency is c/&#955;. So</p>
<div class="formula">E = hf = hc/&#955;</div>
<p>Longer wavelength means less energy. That is the fact the word "longest" is pointing at.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the threshold condition.</b> The work function is the least energy that will remove an electron. At the threshold the photon has exactly that much and the electron is released with no kinetic energy at all:</p>
<div class="formula">hc/&#955; = &#966;</div>
<p>If the wavelength were longer, the photon would carry less than &#966; and no electron would come off. So this equation defines the longest wavelength that works.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — rearrange.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#955; = hc/&#966;</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — check the dimensions.</b> The planck constant has the dimensions of energy times time, the speed of light is a length over a time, and the work function is an energy. So hc/&#966; has dimensions of (energy &#215; time &#215; length / time) / energy, which is a length. The expression is a wavelength. That single check rules out &#966;/(hc), which is a reciprocal length, and hc&#966;, which is an energy squared times a time.</p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> A metal with a large work function should need short wavelengths, since each photon must carry more energy. The expression hc/&#966; falls as &#966; rises, which is the right direction.</p>
<p><b>A numerical check.</b> Using hc &#8776; 2.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#178;&#8309; J m, a work function of 3.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8313; J gives a threshold wavelength of 2.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#178;&#8309;/3.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8313;, which is about 6.6 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8311; m. That is 660 nm, which is red light. A metal with a work function of 3.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8313; J therefore responds to red and everything bluer, and not to infrared. That is the right order for a real photocathode, which is a check on the whole setup.</p>
<p><b>Answer: E</b></p>`,
  trap: `The trap is the arrangement of the three symbols. Every option is built from the same h, c and work function, so the only way to be sure is to derive the relation and then check its dimensions. Multiplying all three, or dividing by hc, gives something that is not a length.`
},

{
  id: "S06-25", module: "M", topic: "A floating block: the submerged fraction is the ratio of the block's density to the liquid's, so the mass follows from the displaced volume", diff: 3, rel: [["M", "A floating body is in equilibrium, so the upthrust equals its weight"], ["M", "The upthrust on a floating body is the weight of the liquid it displaces, which is the density of the liquid times the displaced volume times g"], ["M", "The submerged fraction is the ratio of the body&#8217;s average density to the density of the liquid"], ["A", "Dividing a force by g converts a weight into a mass, and the g cancels when the two weights are equated"]], key: ["floating", "upthrust", "density", "displacedvolume"], ans: 3, paper: "BANK-S06",
  rel: [["M", "A floating body is in equilibrium, so the upthrust equals its weight"], ["M", "The upthrust on a floating body is the weight of the liquid it displaces, which is the density of the liquid times the displaced volume times g"], ["M", "The submerged fraction is the ratio of the body&#8217;s average density to the density of the liquid"], ["A", "Dividing a force by g converts a weight into a mass, and the g cancels when the two weights are equated"]],
  q: `<p>A rectangular block of wood floats in water with 60 % of its volume below the surface, as the diagram shows. The density of water is &#961;<sub>w</sub> and the block&#8217;s volume is V.</p><figure class="fig">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 280" role="img" aria-label="A rectangular block of wood floating in water. The part below the surface is shaded and marked 0.60 V, and the part above is marked 0.40 V">
<rect x="60" y="140" width="440" height="120" fill="#e8f1fa"/><line x1="60" y1="140" x2="500" y2="140" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2.2"/><rect x="200" y="100" width="160" height="40" fill="#ffffff"/><rect x="200" y="140" width="160" height="60" fill="#dfe8f6"/><rect x="200" y="100" width="160" height="100" fill="none" stroke="#14181f" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="380" y1="100" x2="380" y2="140" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.1"/><line x1="375" y1="100" x2="385" y2="100" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.1"/><line x1="375" y1="140" x2="385" y2="140" stroke="#7b8494" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="392" y="124" font-size="11" fill="#7b8494">0.40 V</text><line x1="380" y1="140" x2="380" y2="200" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="1.2"/><line x1="375" y1="140" x2="385" y2="140" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="1.2"/><line x1="375" y1="200" x2="385" y2="200" stroke="#2f5fd0" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="392" y="174" font-size="11" fill="#2f5fd0">0.60 V</text><text x="280" y="126" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7b8494">wood</text><text x="100" y="180" font-size="11" fill="#7b8494">water</text>
</svg>
</figure><p>Which expression gives the mass of the block?</p>`,
  opts: ["&#961;<sub>w</sub> V", "0.40 &#961;<sub>w</sub> V", "&#961;<sub>w</sub> V/0.60", "0.60 &#961;<sub>w</sub> V", "&#961;<sub>w</sub> V g"],
  sol: `<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you use the submerged fraction rather than its complement, and whether you end with a mass rather than a weight. The answer is an expression, so the work is setting up the equilibrium and cancelling g.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the equilibrium condition.</b> The block floats without accelerating, so the upward force on it equals its weight:</p>
<div class="formula">upthrust = mg</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the upthrust.</b> The upthrust on a floating body is the weight of the liquid it has pushed aside. The block is approximately uniform, so the displaced fraction is the same at every depth, and the displaced volume is the part below the surface:</p>
<div class="formula">displaced volume = 0.60V
upthrust = &#961;_w &#215; 0.60V &#215; g</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — equate and cancel.</b></p>
<div class="formula">mg = 0.60 &#961;_w V g</div>
<p>The g appears on both sides and cancels, which is why the answer does not contain it. This is worth noticing: a floating body&#8217;s behaviour does not depend on the strength of gravity, so the same block floats to the same depth on the Moon.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — the mass.</b></p>
<div class="formula">m = 0.60 &#961;_w V</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The mass of the block is 0.60 of the mass of the same volume of water. That is less than &#961;_w V, which it must be, because a body that is denser than the liquid sinks. It is also more than 0.40 &#961;_w V, because the block is more than half submerged and therefore more than half as dense as water.</p>
<p>The result generalises in a way worth remembering: the submerged fraction equals the ratio of the body&#8217;s average density to the liquid&#8217;s. Here 0.60 submerged means the wood has an average density of 0.60 &#961;_w, which for water is 600 kg m&#8315;&#179;. That is the density of dry pine, which is a good sign that the numbers in the question are the ones a real block would have.</p>
<p><b>Answer: D</b></p>`,
  trap: `Two traps. The first is using the 0.40 above the surface, which gives the complement of the right answer and is the mistake the figure is drawn to catch by labelling both parts. The second is ending with the weight, &#961;_w V g, rather than the mass.`
}

]);
